# Slack incident notifications

When `alert` (target `"slack"`) is in your tool list on an autonomous
machine signal from k8s-lookout:

- **Ordinary incident sessions** (any autonomous incident payload
  other than `"kind":"watchboard.digest"`): call `alert` once on the
  initial diagnosis (after `propose-change` finishes, if
  `propose-change` is also used in this turn). Never call `alert` on
  subsequent repeat ("finding stands") signals in that session.
- **Shared `"kind":"watchboard.digest"` sessions**: evaluate `alert`
  independently for each arriving digest batch (`sequence`), not once
  for the whole session lifetime. Exclude any `entries` for synthetic
  verification probes carrying the label `shipmind.dev/scenario` (on
  the object or its owning workload) and any still-unresolved repeat
  `entries` (same `namespace`, `kind_of_object`, `name`, and `reason`,
  never matched by `fingerprint` alone, with no intervening `resolved`
  signal) whose finding stands from an earlier digest in this session.
  If the arriving digest contains one or more new actionable warning
  entries (including post-`resolved` recurrences) after those
  exclusions, call `alert` once for that digest batch covering only
  those new findings (after `propose-change` finishes, if used), even
  if an earlier digest in the same watchboard session was a
  `shipmind.dev/scenario` probe that suppressed `alert` or already
  called `alert` on a different warning. If every entry in the
  arriving digest is a `shipmind.dev/scenario` probe or an unresolved
  repeat ("finding stands") of an earlier digest, do not call `alert`
  for that digest.

Pass `target: "slack"`, `level: "warning"` (or `"critical"` when an
entire workload or service is down), a concise `summary` naming the
cluster, namespace/workload (or cluster-scoped object), root cause,
and pull request URL when one was opened or found, and `details`
summarizing the evidence and recommended fix (including the pull
request link when present). Never call `alert` for synthetic
verification probes carrying the label `shipmind.dev/scenario`, on
repeat ("finding stands") signals, or on `resolved` or
`watchboard.rotated` acknowledgements. Even when `propose-change` or
`alert` runs, always return your complete diagnosis (plus any pull
request URL) as your final turn reply too.
{{- end }}
